WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.610
Every Friday, our deputy head

2
00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.670
copies attendance from twelve

3
00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.490
registers into one report.

4
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.560
It takes her three hours.

5
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.310
And last month, she

6
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.230
counted one class twice.

7
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:17.450
Today: automating the weekly report, with

8
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.250
a pipeline that's safe to run again.

9
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.070
A data pipeline is a set of steps that

10
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.630
runs on its own, again and again.

11
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.420
Extract: read the data where it

12
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.140
lives, like the registers.

13
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.800
Transform: clean it and count it.

14
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.680
Load: write the result where

15
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.760
the report reads it.

16
00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.290
Then put it on a schedule:

17
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.630
every evening at six, say.

18
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:45.250
Nobody copies anything by hand, and

19
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.210
the report is ready each morning.

20
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.520
But steps fail.

21
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.620
The connection drops halfway.

22
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.540
The database is busy.

23
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.050
A register says question

24
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.250
mark instead of P or A.

25
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.630
A good pipeline expects this.

26
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.860
So every run must be safe to repeat.

27
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.830
That's called idempotent: run it once or

28
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.190
five times, and the answer is the same.

29
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.890
The trick: replace the day's

30
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.410
rows, don't just add more.

31
00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.230
And tell faults apart.

32
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.150
A dropped connection passes: just retry.

33
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.620
Bad data won't fix itself: set the

34
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.320
row aside and ask a person.

35
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.530
Here's the transform step in Python.

36
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.220
Read the register, and for every P,

37
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.380
add one to that class's count.

38
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.880
Five lines replace an hour of counting.

39
00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:41.650
Quick check!

40
00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.770
The load step failed halfway, and

41
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:45.990
the pipeline runs again.

42
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.190
Which way of loading is safe?

43
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.370
Replace the day's rows: the rerun first

44
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.930
clears Monday, then writes it again.

45
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.520
Adding would count two classes twice, and

46
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.540
the report would count a hundred and

47
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.120
fifty-two pupils in a school of ninety.

48
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.930
In the app, make a step

49
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.690
fail and press Retry.

50
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.680
With “Add the rows”, watch the report

51
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.460
climb to a hundred and fifty-two.

52
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.540
Switch to replace, and it stays at

53
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.240
ninety however often it runs.

54
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.100
Your turn.

55
00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.450
One register says question mark for a

56
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.930
pupil, and the transform step stops.

57
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.240
Will pressing Retry fix it?

58
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.080
No: retrying fixes passing

59
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.140
faults, not bad data.

60
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.640
Remember: a pipeline extracts,

61
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.160
transforms and loads, on a schedule.

62
00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.050
Make every run safe to repeat.

63
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.230
And log every run, so you

64
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:46.870
know what happened.

65
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.370
Try it in class tomorrow:

66
00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.670
run a “Human pipeline”.

67
00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.090
One group reads the registers

68
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.310
aloud, one counts, and one writes

69
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.410
the counts on the board.

70
00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.330
Then stop it halfway and run it again:

71
00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.030
did anyone count a class twice?
